{
  "answer_predetermined": false,
  "worthwhile": true,
  "derivation": "No algebraic identity fixes the outcome. The intervention adds a Frobenius-norm consistency regularization term (coeff=0.01) between block 0 and block 1 query projection weights to the training objective. Whether this improves subject-disjoint generalization depends on: (1) whether deeper layers benefit from or are constrained by matching shallower attention patterns, (2) the actual data distribution in UCI HAR, (3) optimization dynamics under this specific regularization. The mechanistic rationale explicitly acknowledges competing possibilities: consistency may prevent overfitting to subject-specific temporal features OR limit representational capacity needed for refinement. Prior evidence shows inconclusive results (primary CI: [-0.0031, 0.0056], replication CI: [-0.0049, 0.0018], both including zero and failing to exceed the 0.0125 margin), confirming genuine empirical uncertainty remains.",
  "reason": "Valid controlled replication with genuine empirical uncertainty. Prior studies were inconclusive (CIs span zero, effect estimates near null), the intervention effect is not algebraically determined by the protocol, alternative outcomes are explicitly registered, and fresh independent sampling can resolve whether a real but hard-to-detect effect exists versus no meaningful effect. This addresses stated uncertainty about stability of prior findings under fresh sampling."
}

