      "id": 1,
      "replacement": "This study assessed a test of cross-layer query consistency loss, which regularizes deeper attention query projection weights toward shallower layer weights, to determine if it improves held-out classification accuracy, with inconclusive results in primary and confirmatory cohorts."
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "replacement": "We provide a rigorous design justification for the subject-disjoint split and paired-seed analysis, distinguishing training-seed replication from the pre-specified confirmatory replication cohort on disjoint seeds."
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "replacement": "The intervention adds a loss term (coefficient=0.01) computed as squared element-wise difference, detached from block 0."
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "replacement": "Scope is the full weight matrix (64, 64) treated as flat parameter space."
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "replacement": "The subject-disjoint split prevents the model from memorizing subject-specific features, avoiding inflated accuracy estimates and enabling tests of robustness to movement styles or sensor characteristics."
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "replacement": "The confirmatory cohort is not an independent replication but the pre-specified confirmatory replication cohort on disjoint seeds."
    },
    {
      "id": 7,
      "replacement": "The measured outcome licenses the conclusion that the intervention does not improve held-out classification accuracy by the target margin."
    },
    {
      "id": 8,
      "replacement": "They conflict with the hypothesis that cross-layer consistency generally improves stability, though *Double Consistency Regularization for Transformer Networks* [17] focused on small-scale training data overfitting rather than subject-disjoint generalization."
    },
    {
      "id": 9,
      "replacement": "Confirmatory replication on different subject-disjoint time-series benchmarks would test transferability."
    },
    {
      "id": 10,
      "replacement": "This study evaluated a test of cross-layer query consistency loss on subject-disjoint time-series classification, with inconclusive results in the primary and confirmatory cohorts."
    },
    {
      "id": 11,
      "replacement": "The outcome suggests that layer-specific adaptation may remain essential for this architecture class in this regime."
    }
  ]
}

